<section class="section section--tight" aria-labelledby="money-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="money-title">Three kinds of money coming in</h2>
      <p>Bank credits in a law practice are not all income. Record each kind against the matter and the client.</p>
    </header>
    <div class="deliver-grid">
      <article><h3>Fees</h3><p>Your professional income. Raise a fee note, even if you are paid in advance, and keep it by matter.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Client expenses</h3><p>Court fees, stamp paper and travel that you pay for a client. Ask your accountant how to record the recovery, because the treatment depends on the arrangement.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Money held for a client</h3><p>For example, settlement amounts or deposits. This is not your income. Keep it clearly identifiable, and take advice on how to hold it.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="gst-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="gst-title">GST: reverse charge changes who pays</h2>
      <p>Under Notification 13/2017-Central Tax (Rate), as amended and explained in a government press release and commentary, GST on legal services by an individual advocate, a senior advocate or a firm of advocates supplied to a business entity is generally paid by the client under reverse charge. Services to individuals who are not business entities are treated differently.</p>
      <p class="para">Non-legal services, such as tax filing or accounting provided by a law firm, may fall outside this rule. Have your receipts reviewed if you offer anything beyond legal work. Check the current text of the notification.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>What your fee note should show</h2>
      <ul class="tick-list plain-list">
        <li>Your name or firm's name, and the date</li>
        <li>Client and matter</li>
        <li>Professional fees, stated separately from any expenses</li>
        <li>Whether the client is a business entity, if relevant to GST</li>
        <li>Any TDS the client tells you it will deduct</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="para">Check the format with your accountant, and any rules that apply to you as an advocate.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="rec-title">
  <div class="wrap layout-side">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="rec-title" class="first-h2">Records that save trouble</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>A matter list:</strong> client, fee agreed, billed, received and outstanding.</li>
        <li><strong>TDS by client:</strong> corporate clients may deduct tax. Match it against your annual statement before you file.</li>
        <li><strong>Office costs:</strong> clerk's pay, chamber rent, books, subscriptions and travel, kept apart from personal costs.</li>
        <li><strong>Cash receipts:</strong> receipted and recorded the same day.</li>
        <li><strong>Partners or associates:</strong> a clear record of shares and payments to each.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="tax">Income tax questions to raise</h2>
      <p>Legal is a specified profession under section 62(4) of the Income-tax Act, 2025, so books must be kept. Section 58 offers a presumptive basis of 50% of receipts for a resident individual or firm (not an LLP), up to ₹50 lakh, or ₹75 lakh if cash receipts are within 5%. Ask whether it suits you, and about advance tax.</p>

      <h2 id="confidential">Confidentiality</h2>
      <p>Your books contain client names and matters. Tell your accountant what must not be shared, use narrations that do not reveal case details, and agree how files are stored. Your own professional duties continue when you outsource.</p>

      <h2 id="ask">What to ask an accountant</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>How will you separate fees, client expenses and client money?</li>
        <li>How do you treat reverse charge when billing business clients?</li>
        <li>Can you report outstanding fees by matter?</li>
        <li>How do you protect client confidentiality?</li>
      </ul>

